-- title:
--   Both branch reductions share a value at the node (a,a^q)
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, let $k$ be a field of characteristic $q$ with decidable equality, and let $red : A \to k$ be a ring homomorphism from the underlying subring of $A$. Let $a \in k$ satisfy $a^{q^2} = a$, and let $f$ belong to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,(1\cdot q)$, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the level-$(1\cdot q)$ modular function field, realised inside the Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$. Assume $f$ lies in $\mathrm{modularLocalizedAtPoint}\,(1\cdot q)$ for the data $A$, $red$, $a$, $a^q$: there are two-variable polynomials $r,s$ over $A$ with the reduction of $s$ nonvanishing at $(a,a^q)$ and $f \cdot (\text{value of } s \text{ at } (j_q, j_{q,N}))$ equal to the corresponding value of $r$. The conclusion asserts: $f$ lies in $\mathrm{modularLocalized}\,(1\cdot q)$, the localisation of the subring generated by the constant series from $A$ together with $\mathrm{jqModC}$ and $\mathrm{jqNModC}$ of level $1\cdot q$ away from the kernel of reduction; the image of $f$ under $\mathrm{modularRedLocHom}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ 1 = k(\mathrm{jqModC}\,k, \mathrm{jqNModC}\,k\,1)$; the same two facts hold for $\mathrm{frickeInvolutionBar}\,(1\cdot q)\,f$, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the chosen Fricke automorphism applied to $f$; and there is a single $c \in k$ such that the first component of $\mathrm{frobNodePair}\ q\ a$, the place attached to the point $a$, takes the value $c$ on the reduction of $f$, while the second component, the place attached to $a^q$, takes the value $c$ on the reduction of the Fricke transform of $f$. Here a place $v$ takes the value $c$ on $g$ when $g$ lies in the valuation subring of $v$ and the residue of $g$ equals the image of $c$ in the residue field.
--
--   This is the elementary half of the crossing analysis for the reduction of $X_0(q)$ modulo $q$, resting on Kronecker's congruence $j_q \equiv j^q$ and on $a \in \mathbb F_{q^2}$: the two branches $(j,j_q) \mapsto (j,j^q)$ and $(j,j_q)\mapsto (j^q,j)$, evaluated at $a$ and at $a^q$ respectively, both land at the plane point $(a,a^q)$, so every function regular at that point acquires the same value along both branches. It feeds the statements about node-localised functions and the existence of common values at the Frobenius node pair, in particular the results on branch vanishing and on crossing presentations.
